# panfrost: don't treat unknown system memory as zero memory
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/44297 state=closed
# ### What does this MR do and why?

<!--Describe in detail what your merge request does and why.-->

```
panfrost: don't treat unknown system memory as zero memory

panfrost_can_create_resource() backs the proxy texture test. It asked
os_get_total_physical_memory() for a budget and returned false when the
platform could not report one, which the GL frontend turns into
GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY without attempting the allocation. Every texture then
fails regardless of size:

    GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY in glTexImage2D(image too large: 8 x 8 x 1, GL_RGBA format)

Only textures consult this path, so buffers and renderbuffers keep
working, and nothing is logged because the refusal happens before any
allocation is attempted.

Return true when the budget is unknown and let the allocation decide. The
separate os_get_total_physical_memory() call goes away with it:
os_get_gpu_heap_size() already makes that call and returns 0 on failure.

The same zero reached panfrost_init_compute_caps(), where MIN2() then
advertised max_mem_alloc_size and max_global_size as 0. Fall back to the
VA range, which is a real bound. caps->video_memory is left alone; zero
is honest there and it is informational.

Found on a bare-metal target whose libc provides sysconf() but not
_SC_PHYS_PAGES, which is a glibc extension rather than POSIX. meson probes
only whether sysconf() exists, so HAVE_SYSCONF selects the sysconf() path
unconditionally; with the constant supplied by a port header, the call
returns -1 there. Any libc with the function but not the constant lands
here. No ordinary Linux configuration does, but stubbing
sysconf(_SC_PHYS_PAGES) to -1 reproduces the refusal on any device.

Tested with a cross build for aarch64 and four EGL applications on a host
device model, where the memory size IS reported: unchanged. That does not
reach the new branch, so it shows no regression rather than the fix
working.

Cc: mesa-stable
```
--- src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_resource.c
+++ src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_resource.c
@@ -1002,13 +1002,19 @@ panfrost_can_create_resource(struct pipe_screen *screen,
                                     template->format, 0))
       return false;
 
-   uint64_t system_memory;
-   if (!os_get_total_physical_memory(&system_memory))
-      return false;
-
    const float heap_memory_percent = pan_screen(screen)->heap_memory_percent;
    uint64_t memory = os_get_gpu_heap_size(heap_memory_percent, NULL);
 
+   /* Zero means we could not find out how much memory the system has,
+    * not that there is none. This is only a hint used by the GL frontend
+    * to reject a resource before trying to allocate it, so an unknown
+    * budget must not become a budget of zero: that rejects every
+    * resource at every size, and the caller reports it as
+    * GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY. Let the allocation decide instead.
+    */
+   if (!memory)
+      return true;
+
    return tmp.plane.layout.data_size_B <= memory;
 }
 

--- src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_screen.c
+++ src/gallium/drivers/panfrost/pan_screen.c
@@ -660,9 +660,15 @@ panfrost_init_compute_caps(struct panfrost_screen *screen)
    uint64_t user_va_end =
       pan_clamp_to_usable_va_range(dev->kmod.dev, PAN_VA_USER_END);
 
-   /* We cannot support more than the VA limit */
-   caps->max_global_size =
-   caps->max_mem_alloc_size = MIN2(available_ram, user_va_end - user_va_start);
+   /* We cannot support more than the VA limit. available_ram is zero when
+    * the system memory size could not be determined; clamping to it would
+    * advertise a maximum allocation of zero, so fall back to the VA limit
+    * alone, which is a real hardware bound either way.
+    */
+   uint64_t va_size = user_va_end - user_va_start;
+
+   caps->max_global_size = caps->max_mem_alloc_size =
+      available_ram ? MIN2(available_ram, va_size) : va_size;
 
    caps->max_local_size = 32768;
    caps->max_clock_frequency = 800; /* MHz -- TODO */

